# Copy to `.env` for local development. Vite only exposes VITE_-prefixed vars.

# Where breakdown reports are delivered.
#
# Get the key at https://web3forms.com — enter the inbox the reports should
# arrive in, confirm the address, and paste the key it mails you here. Then add
# the same value as a repository secret named WEB3FORMS_KEY (Settings →
# Secrets and variables → Actions) so the deployed build picks it up.
#
# This key is PUBLIC: it ships in the client bundle, as it must for a static
# site, and can only ever cause mail to be sent to the address it was issued
# for. The env var exists so the destination can change without a code edit.
#
# Leave it unset and the form falls back to composing the report in the
# visitor's own mail client — it never silently discards a report.
VITE_WEB3FORMS_KEY=

# The content backend: the Supabase project holding the projects, the spare
# parts, their photos, and the admin panel's logins. See "Content & admin
# panel" in the README for setting it up.
#
# Both are behind the "Connect" button at the top of the project dashboard.
# Otherwise: the URL (https://<project-id>.supabase.co) is under Project
# Settings → Data API, and the key under Project Settings → API Keys. The key
# is the PUBLISHABLE one (sb_publishable_…) — or the legacy "anon" key, which
# works the same. Like
# the Web3Forms key it is public by design and ships in the bundle: what it
# can reach is decided by the database's row-level security, which lets it
# read published rows and nothing else.
#
# NEVER put the secret / service_role key here, or anywhere in this repo.
#
# Add the same two values as repository secrets named SUPABASE_URL and
# SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY for the deployed build.
#
# Leave them unset and the site renders the snapshot committed in
# src/content/snapshot, and /admin says it has nothing to connect to.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
